"""spec.py RPC-output wiring template for a semantic-layer data product.

Copy the `data_product_rpc_output()` block below into your spec.py, filling in
the infra-profile service ref and data product name.  This template is itself
the canonical reference for the exact call shapes.

Required imports (add to your spec.py):
    from nxd.spec import data_product_rpc_output, rpc_function, rpc_server, code

The semantic kit imports (add to your spec.py):
    from semantic import build_semantic_tools
    from registry import REGISTRY

WIRING PATTERN — the only correct way to expose MCP tools in NXD:

    _rpc = data_product_rpc_output()
    for _t in build_semantic_tools(REGISTRY):
        _rpc = _rpc.function(
            rpc_function(code(_t.fn), _t.request_model, _t.response_model)
            .description(_t.description)
        )
    _rpc = _rpc.port(
        "mcp-api",
        rpc_server("{{infra_profile_mcp_service_ref}}")
        .enable_endpoints()
        .mcp_path("/mcp"),
    )

    spec = (
        data_product(
            name="{{dp_name}}",
            domain="{{domain}}",
            description="{{description}}",
            version="0.1.0",
            infra_profile="{{infra_profile}}",
        )
        .output(_rpc)
        # Add inputs, transforms, storage outputs, etc. as needed.
    )

SERVICE REF FORM:
    "/infra-profile/<INFRA_PROFILE_NAME>#/services/<service-name>"
    e.g. "/infra-profile/ecommerce-demo#/services/snowflake-mcp"

IMPORTANT:
- `data_product_rpc_output()` is the ONLY supported MCP delivery mechanism.
- `rpc_function(code(t.fn), t.request_model, t.response_model)` requires ALL
  THREE positional arguments — omitting request_model or response_model raises
  a TypeError at spec-build time.
- `.description(t.description)` is chainable on rpc_function and sets the MCP
  tool description visible to agents.
- `.enable_endpoints()` publishes the HTTP+MCP endpoint on the rpc_server.
- `.mcp_path("/mcp")` sets the MCP mount path.
- Never pass the SemanticTool list to anything else — it is only meaningful when
  iterated and wired via rpc_function as shown above.
"""
